Because the code is just running deterministic checks over the stored voyage data.

Think of it like a rules engine:

```ts
if required document is missing:
  create missing_document job

if compliance risk_score >= 70:
  create watchlist_risk / ai_action_plan job

if event severity is high/medium/watch:
  create watchlist_event job

if payment words appear in emails/docs:
  create payment_risk job

if PDA exists but FDA is missing:
  create pda_fda_comparison job

if bunker report numbers don’t match bunker docs:
  create reconciliation_conflict job
```

No model has to “understand” anything. It’s string matching, date/number comparisons, severity checks, and missing-record checks.

For example, missing document jobs come from [workflows.ts](/Users/socrates/Documents/syntheci-shipping/packages/shared/src/workflows.ts:174):

```ts
const available = new Set(context.documents.map(...));

return requiredDocumentRules
  .filter((rule) => rule.required(context) && !available.has(rule.type))
  .map((rule) => ({
    jobType: "missing_document",
    title: `Missing ${rule.label} for ${context.voyage.id}`,
  }));
```

The misleading one is this: [intelligence.ts](/Users/socrates/Documents/syntheci-shipping/packages/shared/src/intelligence.ts:160)

```ts
export function buildAiActionPlanJobs(...)
```

Despite the name, it still just does:

```ts
if (context.complianceFlag && context.complianceFlag.riskScore >= 70) {
  jobs.push({
    jobType: "ai_action_plan",
    title: `AI action plan for ${context.voyage.id}`,
  });
}
```

So the jobs appear because the app has enough structured/demo data to infer them with plain code. The “AI” label is basically marketing/naming right now, not an LLM call.

